# The state at a handler is the meet of the states at its throws, so an iteration restarts behind a catch only where nothing that the iteration did before any of those throws can be seen. Three restarts: one of `straight`,
# one in the try of `caught` in front of its throw, and one behind the join with its catch. f2 = straight, f3 = caught
# The net of effects holds each of them to that: the handler puts back the counts of a quiet call that threw, as the code behind the call does where it returns. no-restart-behind-caught-effects.ir holds what may not restart.
